  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nLight, periodic rain showers will continue through the remainder of \nthis afternoon and into the overnight, gradually dissipating \nfrom southwest to northeast. A period of light drizzle is \npossible as moisture wraps around the low in the early morning \nhours of Tuesday. There is potential for fog to develop \novernight along river valleys and low areas. There is \napproximately a 20-30% probability that visibility may briefly \nfall to 2 miles or less. All shower activity should be northeast\nof the CWA by around sunrise, and fog should burn off shortly \nafter. Overnight lows will dip into the upper 30s to mid 40s. \nThick, low stratus clouds linger into early Tuesday afternoon \nbefore being swept away by a second shortwave moving through \nwestern North Dakota. Winds become southerly and increase in \nresponse to the tightened SPG. Gusts of 20-30 mph are possible \nalong and west of the James River Valley, with gusts generally \nless than 20 mph to the east. Shallow mixing will tap into \nstrong WAA in the 925-850 mb layer. At the surface we can expect\nhighs to warm nicely into the low 50s to low 60s. The warmest \ntemperatures are expected over central South Dakota and \nnortheastern Iowa. \n\nWednesday morning lows will be warm for late October, in the 40s to \nlow 50s. Average lows this time of year are in the lower 30s. \nAnother day of light to breezy southerly winds and strong WAA will \nwarm our afternoon highs in the 60s and 70s. Similar to the lows, we \nare quite warm for this time of year when average highs are \ngenerally in the mid 50s. Around mid-morning a broad upper wave and \nweak surface low will take shape over central Canada. An associated \ncold front will drape from there southwest through central South \nDakota into southeast Wyoming. Light rain is expected to develop \nalong the front, mainly west of the James River late in the morning. \nAs the wave and frontal system move southeast rain will move into \nthe I-29 corridor and into western Minnesota and Iowa. Rain is \nexpected to be spotty in nature with little accumulation. Only a few \nhundredths to a tenth or two. Showers clear out to the east after \nmidnight. Behind the front winds become northwesterly and light. \nThis will allow for good radiational cooling and lows will drop into \nthe mid 30s over central South Dakota to the upper 40s east of I-29. \n \nThursday winds aloft will become zonal to south-southwest. At the \nsurface high pressure builds in from the northwest. Winds will turn \nsoutheasterly as that high scoots off to the east, but remain light. \nDry and mostly sunny conditions with highs in the low to mid 60s. \nThe majority of the day Friday will be dry with highs in the 60s. \nFriday late afternoon and into Saturday another shortwave system \nmoves through bringing additional chances for rain. While currently \nrainfall looks to be spotty and light, this could impact outdoor \nHalloween plans. A brief break on Sunday before a much more \npronounced upper trough brings wide spread chances for\naccumulating rainfall to the area Monday into late Tuesday. \nWhile uncertainty is very high at this juncture, long term \nensemble guidance indicates the potential for widespread 0.5 to \n1 inch. Stay tuned as we monitor the trends with this system and\ndetails emerge.",
